// _tests_/bit/benchgate/baselinesmacostail.bit — the second half of the
// macOS/arm64 baseline table (json, map, matrix, sort, strings, trustcache,
// strmap) plus its calibration reasoning, split out of `baselines.bit` by
// #4623 to clear spec/LINT.md:186's 800-line E0200 ceiling (790 of 800).
//
// Sibling of `baselines.bit` in the same directory module: no `import`, one
// shared scope, exactly like `baselinesdefs.bit`, `baselinesflip.bit` and
// `baselineslinux.bit`. This file is pure data, same as its parent, and
// `baselinesMacosArm64()` (still in `baselines.bit`) names every `case*()`
// below directly — one module, so no import and no E0042.
//
// A pure move — declaration order and every line below are unchanged. The
// third split of this table: #3023 cut it out of `benchgate.bit`, #4067 cut
// `Baseline`/`platformName`/`baselinesFor` out to `baselinesdefs.bit`.

// --- #3129: json, map, matrix, sort, strings -------------------------
//
// #3097 added these five to bench/cases/ and recorded no baseline, so the
// five WORST cases in the suite (strings 117x C, json 123x, matrix 109x,
// sort 15.8x, map 11.3x) were the only ones under active optimization
// with no regression guard: #2293, #3107, #3111 and #3116 all landed
// against them on 2026-08-15 with nothing watching.
//
// Measured on `main` @ d4b5e88e (2026-08-15) with the method this file's
// header prescribes -- built with the compiler under test, then 7 direct
// `/usr/bin/time -l` runs each, minimum taken, on a host carrying one
// sibling make-driver build (load average 3.8-4.6). The readings, as
// "cpu centiseconds / peak RSS bytes":
//   json    535/922615808  535/922615808  536/922615808  540/922615808
//           541/922615808  557/922615808  518/922615808
//   map      53/56573952    51/56573952    53/60784640    53/56573952
//            51/56573952    52/60784640    53/56573952
//   matrix  502/33505280   507/33505280   467/33505280   486/33505280
//           497/33505280   503/33505280   502/33505280
//   sort     83/48267264    82/48250880    84/48267264    83/48250880
//            84/48250880    83/48250880    82/48250880
//   strings 337/998424576  343/998424576  355/998424576  383/998424576
//           395/998424576  386/998408192  365/998424576
//
// THE CPU BASELINES BELOW ARE LOWER THAN THAT SERIES' MINIMUM ON FOUR OF
// THE FIVE, and deliberately so: nine further `./make test-benchgate`
// runs (the gate's own min-of-3, load average 2.1-17.7, 0-1 sibling
// make-driver builds) read a lower floor on json, map, matrix and
// strings. The floor is the quantity being estimated and noise can only
// inflate it, so the lowest observed reading wins:
//   json    517 729 575 498 563 520 485 504 481 -> floor 481 (direct 518)
//   map      49  96  61  54  54  53  52  55  50 -> floor  49 (direct  51)
//   matrix  490 543 522 461 480 458 461 472 457 -> floor 457 (direct 467)
//   sort     90  86 115  84  94  82  82  93  83 -> floor  82 (direct  82)
//   strings 392 379 345 364 398 332 335 381 378 -> floor 332 (direct 337)
// The last of those nine was the quietest (load 2.1) and produced the
// lowest json and matrix readings, which is the expected shape: the floor
// is only approached when nothing else is on the box. The remaining gap
// is under 1% on both, far below anything either ceiling turns on.
// RSS over those same 9 runs never once fell below the direct minimum,
// and barely moved at all: json and matrix read ONE value in all 16 runs
// (7 direct + 9 gate); map's gate minimum was 56573952 in all 9, the 2
// direct runs that read 60784640 being the only +7.4% blips anywhere;
// sort took 48267264 in 4 of 16 (+0.03%) and strings 998424576 in 8 of
// 16 (+0.002%). Peak RSS is the load-insensitive metric this file's
// header claims, and the RSS baselines below are consequently the strong
// half of each case's guard; the CPU spread above is not, and it is what
// drives the per-case ceilings recorded below.
//
// gcMinKb is 0 (inherit) on all five, unlike churn: BIT_GC_MIN_KB's
// default is 4096 KiB (runtime/ABI.md:775) and the smallest peak here is
// matrix at 32 MB, so the trigger floor is under 13% of even the smallest
// case's RSS and cannot move these baselines the way it moves churn's
// 1.9 MB one. Pinning it would add a knob that guards nothing. Verified
// rather than assumed: `BIT_GC_MIN_KB=1` on the map binary read 0.57s
// user against 0.57s at the default.
//
// json: CPU ceiling 175 (1.75x = 841cs), not the blanket 150 (721cs).
// The worst of the 9 gate runs above is 729cs -- 1.50x the floor, i.e. a
// reading that would have gone RED at 150 with the code unchanged, and it
// did not come from a small-baseline effect (json's floor is 481cs, 16x
// fib's). The mechanism is this case's concurrent collector: `user`
// EXCEEDS `real` here (4.45s real against 5.99s user on one direct run),
// so user CPU sums the mutator and the collector thread, and contention
// buys the collector more spinning, not less work. 841 clears 729 by
// 112cs (15%) while a genuine 2x regression (962cs) still trips it by
// 121cs -- the same both-sided trade #2982 made for fib.
// objects (#3130): 10200067 = swept 2550040 + live 7650027, identical
// across 5 direct runs (see #3130's ticket comment) -- and matches
// #2293's own measured before/after (15,450,097 -> 10,200,067) exactly.
// cpuCentis 481 -> 295 (#3183, main @ aafaa385). 9 direct runs, boxlock
// solo: 307 296 296 298 297 301 295 303 308 -> min 295 (gate min-of-3 and
// A/B round both read 300). At 481 the 175 ceiling sat 2.80x above actual,
// which is the "guards almost nothing" state this re-baseline exists to
// fix. 295 at 175 is 516cs, clearing the worst of 19 observations (308) by
// 208cs/40%. cpuCeilingPct stays 175 for the reason recorded above -- this
// case's concurrent collector makes `user` exceed `real`, so contention
// buys the collector spinning, not less work.
// #4067: rssBytes 922615808 -> 284164096 (0.308x), objects 10200069 ->
// 6000056 (0.588x). The RSS guard fired only past 1383923712B, 4.87x the real
// cost. objects sat INSIDE the [0.5, 1.05] band throughout, so the two-sided
// ratchet built to catch an unrecorded win missed this one by 8.8 points.
// objects 6000056 -> 5400061 (#4586, main `48dd5501`): a fourth unrecorded
// win the [0.5, 1.05] band absorbed, and `flipDebtViolation` reads this field
// as the flip's baseline -- see `baselinesflip.bit` for the arithmetic.
fn caseJson(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "json",
    expected: "11249925000 2099959 75000 1388890",
    cpuCentis: 295,
    rssBytes: 284164096,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 175,
    objects: 5400061,
  }
}

// map: CPU ceiling 250 (2.5x = 122cs). This is the largest override in
// the file and the reason is measured, not assumed -- `./make
// test-benchgate` FAILED on this case with the code unchanged while this
// table was being calibrated:
//   FAIL benchgate: map user CPU regressed: 96cs (min of 3),
//     baseline 51cs, ceiling 76cs (150%)
// at load average 17.7. That is #2982's fib defect exactly: 49cs of
// absolute baseline leaves 24cs of headroom under a 1.5x ceiling, and
// scheduling noise on this host is roughly fixed in centiseconds rather
// than proportional, so it fills that headroom. The other 8 gate runs
// read 49-61cs.
//
// WHAT THIS COSTS, STATED PLAINLY: at 122cs, map's CPU check can no
// longer see a 2x regression (98cs) -- it catches 2.5x and worse. No
// ceiling can do better here, because a doubling of this case's cost is
// literally smaller than the noise already observed on the unchanged
// binary. Mutated-tested rather than argued (#3129): a 3x-compute map,
// output byte-identical, measured 155cs and FAILED against this 122cs
// ceiling. The honest way to narrow this is to make the case itself
// bigger, which changes `expected` on every platform including
// baselinesLinuxX64 -- rejected here for the same reason #2982 rejected
// re-baselining fib's workload. cpuCentis is still the pre-#4064 49; #4067
// measured 12cs on a quiet box but changed no cpuCentis in this file and
// filed the CPU half separately -- see its policy note on the class above.
//
// #4064 gave map<K,V> a real capacity hint and bench/cases/map/map.bit
// now uses it (map<i64,i64>(n) instead of map<i64,i64>()), pre-sizing the
// table once instead of growing+rehashing through ~18 doublings. RSS was
// the documented fine-grained, load-insensitive detector for this case
// (56573952B identical across 9 runs) and objects likewise (72,
// identical across 5) -- both moved for real, not from noise, confirmed
// by two solo `./make test-benchgate` runs agreeing closely:
//   run 1: rss=38076416B obj=14 (min of 3)
//   run 2: rss=38060032B obj=14 (min of 3)
// rssBytes takes the lower of the two, matching #4056's precedent
// ("the lowest of N readings"); objects is exactly deterministic, per
// #3130, so both runs agreeing at 14 is not a coincidence.
fn caseMap(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "map",
    expected: "7874994750000 1125000 5906250000000 1125000",
    cpuCentis: 49,
    rssBytes: 38060032,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 250,
    objects: 14,
  }
}

// cpuCentis 457 -> 207 (#3752, main @ 3931decd, boxlock solo,
// make-driver=0 and no foreign process >=8% of a core either side). 9
// direct `/usr/bin/time -l` runs: 207 207 209 210 211 210 208 208 208 ->
// min 207, BELOW #3183's clause-removed reference of 216cs. Ceiling stays
// the blanket 150 (310cs), 103cs of absolute headroom. Compute-bound with
// no second thread accruing CPU (`user` ~= `real`), which is why it is
// the steadiest of the five and why a tight ceiling here is worth keeping.
// objects (#3130): 50 = swept 26 + live 24, identical across 5 direct
// runs (see #3130's ticket comment).
//
// #4056 RE-RECORDED objects 52 -> 16 and rssBytes 33505280 -> 8568832.
// NOT a runtime improvement and must not be read as one: matrix.bit
// allocated its three 512x512 f64 buffers INSIDE the 6-trial loop where
// matrix.go and matrix.c allocate them once outside it, so this side
// built 18 matrices to their 3. Hoisting them (plus the explicit c-zeroing
// pass the other two already pay) is what moved both numbers -- the case
// is measuring the same program on all three sides for the first time.
//   objects  46 -> 16, exactly deterministic per this file's own header;
//            18 slices x 2 objects (descriptor + buffer) became 3 x 2.
//            The 46 read on main is itself below the recorded 52, so the
//            old value had already drifted; 16 is what the gate reads.
//   rssBytes lowest of 18 readings (15 direct `/usr/bin/time -l` +
//            3 gate reps): 8568832. 14 of the 15 direct runs read
//            8585216, which is also what the gate's min-of-3 reported --
//            peak RSS on this case is effectively a single value, as this
//            file's header claims for the metric generally. The old
//            33505280 was a dead ceiling: 3.9x the value it guarded.
// cpuCentis DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED at 207. Cycles moved -1.01% (7.849G ->
// 7.770G, trimmed mean of 15, distributions non-overlapping) -- the
// zeroing pass costs about what the 15 removed allocations saved, so
// there is no CPU improvement to record. The gate read 196cs on this
// branch, but on a box carrying a foreign process at 106% of a core; that
// is not the quiet-box floor #3752 measured 207 with, and lowering a
// load-sensitive baseline from a contended run is how a false ceiling
// gets enshrined.
fn caseMatrix(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "matrix",
    expected: "38654738429 75498279 24453 24856",
    cpuCentis: 207,
    rssBytes: 8568832,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 16,
  }
}

// sort: CPU ceiling 180 (1.8x = 147cs), not 150 (123cs). Worst of the 9
// gate runs is 115cs, 1.40x the floor -- 8cs under a 150 ceiling, which
// is one scheduling hiccup from red and is the same "sitting AT the
// ceiling once" state #2982 refused to ship for fib. 82cs of baseline is
// small enough for the absolute-headroom mechanism to bite (41cs at 150,
// against the 14cs that broke fib). 147 clears the worst reading by 32cs
// (28%, matching the margin #2982 accepted) and still trips a 2x
// regression (164cs) by 17cs -- a wider both-sided margin than fib's own
// 190, which sits only 3cs under its 2x point.
// objects (#3130): 2200045, identical across 5 direct runs -- but, like
// churn above, swept/live individually varied between runs (e.g. swept
// 1816690/live 383355 vs swept 1816686/live 383359) while their sum did
// not (see #3130's ticket comment for all 5 readings).
//
// objects 2200047 -> 600026 (#4055). This case builds 150,000 short
// strings through std/strings' Builder, and `newBuilder()` used to start
// its backing array at the null header, so `append`'s doubling-from-1
// charged 8.33 heap objects to produce one ~8-character string. Starting
// that array at 16 bytes makes it a flat 4.00 for any string up to 16.
// The case's other 1,250,022 objects (the 800k i64 half and the string
// sort itself) are untouched, so the whole delta is 150,000 x 4.33.
//
// 600026 is EXACT, not a floor: swept=428263 live=171763 in all three
// direct runs, and the gate's own min-of-3 read the same. That is why
// this guard is two-sided -- the old 2200047 was already 1.76x the tree
// it was protecting (main measured 1250029 here), which is how an
// improvement hides until the next one pushes it under the 50% floor.
// Recorded rather than widened: the ratchet only works if it is re-cut.
//
// #4067 is that ticket: rssBytes 48250880 -> 32702464 (0.678x); objects
// unchanged at 600026, re-confirmed 30/30. sort is this table's noisiest RSS
// case, the only one spanning more than two pages (one reading at 32899072,
// +0.601% over the median), which 1.5x still clears by two orders.
fn caseSort(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "sort",
    expected: "29402557 true 781018614 true",
    cpuCentis: 82,
    rssBytes: 32702464,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 180,
    objects: 600026,
  }
}

// strings keeps the blanket 150 (498cs): worst of the 9 gate runs is
// 398cs, 1.20x the floor, 100cs (25%) of margin. It shares json's
// concurrent-collector shape (`user` 4.23s against `real` 3.16s) and is
// measurably noisier than matrix because of it, but its floor is high
// enough in absolute terms that 1.5x still absorbs the spread. This is
// the suite's worst case against C (117x) and #2293's target, so it keeps
// the strictest bar the data supports.
// objects (#3130): 7125042 = swept 6682506 + live 442536, identical
// across 5 direct runs (see #3130's ticket comment).
// cpuCentis 332 -> 164 (#3183, main @ aafaa385). 9 direct runs, boxlock
// solo: 176 180 175 181 177 175 168 172 174 -> min 168; a separate A/B
// round on the same binary read 164 and the gate's min-of-3 read 170, so
// the floor across 19 observations is 164. Ceiling stays the blanket 150
// (246cs), clearing the worst observation (181) by 65cs/36%.
// #4067: rssBytes 998408192 -> 137216000 (0.137x), objects 7125044 ->
// 7125035. The worst headroom in the file -- the guard fired only past
// 1497612288B, 10.91x the real cost -- and it had fallen further still since
// #3183 observed 193101824B here.
fn caseStrings(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "strings",
    expected: "28500007 0 10000",
    cpuCentis: 164,
    rssBytes: 137216000,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 7125035,
  }
}

// --- #2948: trustcache -----------------------------------------------
//
// Regression probe for #2298's TLS trust-store cache
// (stdlib/http/trustcache.bit): std/http's newClient() reaches
// defaultClientConfig() -> defaultRoots() -> defaultTrustRoots() on
// every construction. Before #2298 that call chain re-parsed the whole
// OS certificate bundle (X.509 + key material) from scratch every time
// -- ~170ms for ~128 macOS roots, trustcache.bit's own header; after
// #2298 only the FIRST call parses and every later call decodes a
// small process-wide byte cache. bench/cases/trustcache/trustcache.bit
// builds 40 Clients back to back via newClient().
//
// WHY OBJECTS (#3130), NOT CPU, IS THE SIGNAL THAT MATTERS HERE. This
// box was at load ~50 with all 6 boxlock build slots held while this
// was measured (2026-08-23) -- exactly the contended environment
// #2982/#3129 already document as unreliable for a small absolute CPU
// baseline. Object count has no such problem: it is exactly
// deterministic (369070 = swept 358394 + live 10676, identical across
// ALL 10 direct runs below) and a parse-per-call allocates roughly N
// times what one cache-hit decode does, so a defeated cache is not a
// marginal regression here -- it is orders of magnitude, and trips the
// #3130 two-sided [50%,105%] band by a huge margin regardless of host
// load.
//
// CACHED (this tree, unmodified) -- 10 direct `/usr/bin/time -l` runs
// of the built binary, BIT_GC_STATS=1, on the loaded box described
// above: user CPU 33/34/35/36/37/55/57/58/61/63cs (min 33, the
// low-33-37 cluster came once contention briefly eased -- exactly the
// "minimum cannot be inflated by noise" case the file header already
// argues), RSS 16433152/16433152/16433152/16449536/16449536/16449536/
// 16449536/16465920/16465920/16596992 (min 16433152). objects
// 358394+10676=369070 in all 10 runs, no exceptions. expected output
// "40" (the fixed loop count; Client.tls is module-private per SPEC
// Sec 11's field-export rule -- setHeader/tls are the fields, none
// exported -- so this case cannot read the resulting root count back
// out and instead asserts the loop completed all 40 constructions).
//
// MUTATION-TESTED, not just reasoned about. stdlib/http/
// trustcache.bit's defaultTrustRoots() was temporarily changed to
// `return tcUncachedRoots()` unconditionally (cache defeated, every
// call does a full parse), rebuilt, and run 5x the same way: user CPU
// 643/644/653/655/661cs (min 643cs = 19.5x this baseline's 33cs),
// objects 8719354+50291=8769645 in all 5 runs (23.76x this baseline's
// 369070, 22.6x the 105% ceiling) -- output unchanged ("40" every
// run), confirming the mutation is a pure performance regression, not
// a wrong-answer bug the output check would already have caught.
// Reverted (`git checkout --`) and confirmed clean via `git diff
// --stat` before this baseline was recorded.
//
// RSS DOES NOT CATCH THIS DEFECT, stated plainly, same as this file
// already does for map's CPU limits above: the mutant's peak RSS
// (14581760-14614528B) is LOWER than the cached baseline
// (16433152-16596992B), because 189 small collections keep the heap
// smaller than 13 larger ones despite doing 23.76x the allocation
// work. RSS stays in this table (every case carries it unconditionally
// per ceiling()) but objects and CPU carry this case's detection.
//
// cpuCeilingPct 250 (not the blanket 150), for the same reason #2982
// gave fib and #3129 gave map: 33cs is too small an absolute baseline
// for 1.5x (49cs ceiling, 16cs of headroom) to survive real contention
// -- the 10 direct runs above already spanned 33-63cs (1.9x) on THIS
// host today. 250% (82cs) clears the worst observed reading (63cs) by
// 30%, matching the margin convention above, while the real regression
// (643cs) still trips it by 561cs (7.8x over) -- objects is this
// case's tight guard; CPU here is a coarse backstop only.
// cpuCentis 33 -> 19 (#3183, main @ aafaa385). The 33 above was measured
// at load ~50 with all 6 boxlock slots held and its own note says so. On a
// solo box, 9 direct runs: 20 20 20 19 20 19 19 20 20 -> min 19 (gate
// min-of-3 read 21, A/B round 19). cpuCeilingPct stays 250 -- 47cs, still
// 28cs of absolute headroom on a 19cs baseline, and the #2298 regression
// it exists to catch measured 643cs, so it trips by 13.7x either way.
// 1.46x of this is #3564's back-edge preempt clause (mutation: 19 -> 13cs),
// so 19 is honest for aafaa385 and drops again once #3564 is fixed.
// #4067: objects 369072 -> 248000, the drift that note predicted, now 0.672x
// and still inside the band and so still invisible to the ratchet. rssBytes
// 16433152 -> 13123584 (0.799x).
fn caseTrustcache(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "trustcache",
    expected: "40",
    cpuCentis: 19,
    rssBytes: 13123584,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 250,
    objects: 248000,
  }
}

// strmap (#4376): the string-keyed map case, added so the suite reaches
// wyhash and the group probe path at all -- `map` is map<i64,i64> and takes
// the splitmix64 scalar path, so nothing here reached the string one.
//
// RECORDED ON A BOX THAT WAS NOT QUIET: two foreign processes each near 100%
// of a core throughout, under `boxlock run`, never `solo`. cpuCentis is
// therefore an UPPER bound -- it can only make this arm loose, never a false
// red -- and should be re-recorded downward on the next quiet run, per the
// downward-only policy in baselinesdefs.bit. objects and rssBytes are not
// load-sensitive (this file's header) and are recorded normally.
//   objects   400041, identical across 5 direct BIT_GC_STATS=1 runs. 400000
//             keys at one heap object each -- Bit's per-key object is a
//             24-byte string view header over one shared blob, the same
//             one-object-per-key shape strmap.go and strmap.c hold -- plus 41
//             for the blob, the builder's doublings, the key array and boot.
//   rssBytes  min of 20 direct `/usr/bin/time -l` runs: 43646976 (median
//             43663360, one 51904512 outlier, inside the blanket 1.5x).
//   cpuCentis 39. The min of the same 20 direct runs was 41 (full spread 41
//             42 42 42 42 43 43 43 43 43 43 43 43 44 44 44 44 45 45 46); a
//             `./make test-benchgate` run then read 39 as its own min of 3,
//             and the lower of the two is recorded per the downward-only
//             rule -- a baseline the gate has already beaten guards nothing.
//
// CPU ceiling 250 (2.5x = 97cs), not the blanket 150 (58cs), for exactly the
// mechanism caseMap's block above records as measured rather than assumed:
// this host's scheduling noise is roughly FIXED in centiseconds, not
// proportional, and a 39cs baseline leaves 19cs of headroom at 1.5x -- less
// than map had when it read 96cs against a 49cs baseline at load 17.7. 250
// buys 58cs of absolute headroom, close to the 73cs the same override buys
// map. The cost, stated as plainly as it is there: at 97cs this arm cannot
// see a 2x regression (78cs), only 2.5x and worse. objects is the sharp
// instrument for this case -- exact, deterministic and two-sided.
fn caseStrmap(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "strmap",
    expected: "4199979000000 6000000 150000 105000000000 150000",
    cpuCentis: 39,
    rssBytes: 43646976,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 250,
    objects: 400041,
  }
}
